proposals.poster_moment:
About frame 500 (20.8 s):
- Centre, rows ~330-600: the "Perf@1 > 1" emerald block letters, just settled after the bounce, ink burst lines around them.
- Above them, rows ~190-280: the "TritonGym" banner.
- Upper-left: the brass stopwatch, its emerald hand stopped before the gold one.
- Below, rows ~600-830:
  - centre: Evo mid-leap with star eyes, card showing one open hole;
  - left: the emerald dart sitting inside its reference outline, Ref with the green flag;
  - right: the golden Oracle with jaw dropped and laurel over one eye;
  - front-left corner: Flash yanking his towel off.

Nothing sits in front of the letters, and all of it holds in the ~2.1-2.6:1 card crop.

proposals.why_clear:
1. The film's structure is the agent loop. The set is a closed circuit: the gym pun made literal as circuit training on a circuit board. The three stations are the three standardised tools, in the order they run: compile gate, then PyTorch weigh-in, then stopwatch race against the oracle. Every failure physically returns to the LLM for a patch, so after two returns the viewer has learned the loop without any words.
2. The cast is the concepts, as in the picture book, and every design explains its role:
   - speech balloon = language model;
   - the page it writes rolls up and runs, so 'the code is what gets tested';
   - a crumpled page = rejected code;
   - a balance that must sit level within a hairline notch = matches the reference within 0.01;
   - a torch = PyTorch;
   - a brass, hand-wound champion = the hand-tuned oracle;
   - the race is run on the GPU die = kernel latency on a GPU;
   - a stack of 164 sheets = the operators still to write.
3. The rule-of-three conflict (CLANG, a loss by a nose, a win) makes 'agent loops beat one-shot' something the viewer feels rather than reads.
4. The two metrics appear as physical actions at the moments they are earned: Pass@1 as a rubber stamp, Perf@1 > 1 as block letters slammed down on a finish line. No chart, panel or overlay is ever needed.
5. Every beat has one read and one cause-and-reaction, with real acting (a gulp, a sweat drop, a smug wind-up, star eyes). The loop point (taking the next sheet of 164) is itself a fact about the benchmark.
6. All facts come from the context: compile / verify / profile, the PyTorch reference, the <= 0.01 tolerance, the hand-tuned oracle, Pass@1, Perf@1 > 1, 164 = 139 / 13 / 12, iterative refinement, diff edits, and one-shot as the lower bound. No numbers are invented, and no model is named or ranked.

scores.strengths:
B7's poster spec is the most card-literate of the four:
- the letters are centred in rows ~330-600;
- the 'TritonGym' banner sits at rows ~190-280;
- faces are at rows 600-830;
- the hold is at least 1.5 s.

It is the only proposal whose poster carries the name and the payoff together, as Qubrio's comic poster does. Flash (a tall coral exclamation mark with ribbons) and Evo (a ball with a visor) are a strong tall-vs-round pair. The B4 to B5 match cut (f287) goes from magnifier lens to stopwatch dial, a big round shape at the same size and screen position, which is ideal as a centred card transition. The B4 magnifier insert (f248-263) makes a tiny tolerance miss big and readable, and the CLANG in B2 (f114-119) crumples both planes at once. The seam is a continuous camera move, and the fan's period divides the length.
